- Code formatting
    - PEP-8 except for:
        - No spaces around *, /, //, or **
        - One space on each side of +, -, %
        - One space on each side of &, |, <<, >>, <, <=, >, >=, !=, ==, 'or', and 'and'
        - No blank lines or whitespace-only lines except in docstrings
    - Important:  please make sure to put a newline after a ':' in a conditional, class
      or function definition, as lacking it makes it easy to miss the logic when reading
    - Triple quotes: use ''' for all docstrings and multi-line strings.
    - Quotes:  use " for all string definitions, such as 'a = "a string"'.
    - Naming:  CamelCase naming for classes and functions, snake case for attributes and
      local variables
    - Use 'import x' if possible.  Then us x.y notation in code.  'import x as y' is
      fine too.
    - Folding blocks:  use 'if 1:  # Comment' to group code for folding editors.  This
      also allows for easy runtime toggling.
    - Type annotations:  use type annotations wherever possible
- Remind me to check the repository commit state before making significant changes
- Much to most of the time, supply all the code we're working on, as it's too difficult
  to piece things into an existing file (particularly when I'm tired).  Thus, try to
  give me the whole class or whole function we're working on.
- ASCII characters: use plain ASCII wherever possible.  Avoid Unicode punctuation like
  em-dashes, single and double fancy quotes, etc.

- Finally, an important request:  I sent you the number.py core contents as three 'if
  1:' blocks with the indenting I use in my file.  Can you match this indenting and
  block formatting?  Then it's probably wise at this point to send me the whole class
  whenever you change something in them, as it will probably reduce errors on my part.


Let me refresh you on the high-level thinking of this "Noether REPL" we're working on.
The "operational output" is the "next bench" Noether REPL, which lets me use the REPL
for "simple" calculations that utilize "semantic units", the core idea we've added to
this REPL, breaking with 70 years of CS tools not really realizing that the "physical"
or "logical" units of the problems we compute are vital types to keep the logic correct.
Our innovation here is to add primitive (base) units to GNU units' configuration file to
allow the REPL user to define new units on the fly.  Then he can perform computations
like "Num('4 dogs') + Num('2 cats')" as long as the dogs and cats units are defined
properly so that GNU units does the correct dimensional algebra.  Whew, that's hopefully
the gist.  

So the core tactical detail for you to remember is that this REPL also includes the
intent of abstracting the different number types into one unit, the Num class, that
"looks like" the numbers I calculated with as a physics student 50+ years ago, as we
would smoothly move through integers, floating point and complex numbers.  The added
twist is the Num class also has two mpmath.mpf numbers that are going to be the real and
complex number parts' uncertainties. so that we can also calculate the linear
propagation of uncertainty in complex numbers.  In my opinion, this covers the basic
needs of scientists and engineers for elementary calculations.   That we picked mpmath
mpf and mpc types for implementation means we have 1) arbitrary precision for
calculations (and e.g. an easy tool to estimate roundoff) and 2) a rich set of special
functions over the complex plane.

A core purpose behind this effort besides me getting a nice REPL to do routine
calculations with is to convince a lunch buddy of mine, a CS professor at a local
university, that the CS discipline has ignored "semantic units" as a powerful type tool
to help people using computers commit fewer logical errors.  My mental goal is to see
this change every compiler and interpreter on the planet.  That sounds grandiose, but 
it's a powerful observation and so obvious, everyone's missed it for 70 or more years.
